bolton.moderngov.co.uk"}],"recent":[{"date":"2026-09-09","committee":"Executive Cabinet Member for Stronger Communities","time":"6.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://bolton.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-14","committee":"Cabinet","time":"4.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://bolton.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-14","committee":"Executive Cabinet Member Climate Change, Environmental Services and Regulatory Services","time":"6.30 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://bolton.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-15","committee":"Executive Cabinet Member for Health and Wellbeing","time":"11.00 am","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://bolton.moderngov.co.uk"},{"date":"2026-09-16","committee":"Executive Cabinet Member Highways, Transport, Planning and Development","time":"5.00 pm","status":"Confirmed","webcast":false,"url":"https://bolton.moderngov.co.uk"}]},"news":[],"news_drafts":[{"headline":"Environment Agency flood defence grant funding reduced; 2026/27 bids exceeded budget by wider margin than historical trend","lead":"Declining external flood defence funding means the council cannot deliver all identified flood risk management schemes, deferring asset protection during an era of increased climate risk.","iso":"2026-08-19","paper_title":"Highways Capital Programme from 2026-2027 and year end outturn of the 2025-2026 Programme","paper_url":"https://bolton.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s16157/16157.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"2025/26 Maintenance Block overspent; additional funding drawn from other sources to cover variance","lead":"Material maintenance budget overruns indicate either deteriorating asset condition driving reactive work, or poor budget forecasting; either suggests future budget pressures.","iso":"2026-08-19","paper_title":"Highways Capital Programme from 2026-2027 and year end outturn of the 2025-2026 Programme","paper_url":"https://bolton.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s16157/16157.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Bridge and structures asset management budgets acknowledged as inadequate for anything beyond emergency repairs","lead":"Chronic underfunding of preventative bridge maintenance creates a deteriorating asset base and eventual need for emergency capital spending or service disruption.","iso":"2026-08-19","paper_title":"Highways Capital Programme from 2026-2027 and year end outturn of the 2025-2026 Programme","paper_url":"https://bolton.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s16157/16157.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-08-19","committee":"Executive Cabinet Member Highways, Transport, Planning and Development","paper_title":"Highways Capital Programme from 2026-2027 and year end outturn of the 2025-2026 Programme","paper_url":"https://bolton.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s16157/16157.pdf","headline":"Environment Agency flood defence grant funding reduced; 2026/27 bids exceeded budget by wider margin than historical trend","detail":"The six-year EA investment programme ended early in 2025/26, pausing flood defence funding. For 2026/27, bids for flood defence funding exceeded available budget by a greater margin than previously experienced, indicating growing unmet flood management needs.","category":"capital","matters":"Declining external flood defence funding means the council cannot deliver all identified flood risk management schemes, deferring asset protection during an era of increased climate risk.","figures":"EA funding pause in 2025/26; 2026/27 bid oversubscription described as exceeding the historical margin","quote":"Bids for funding during the first year of this period (2026/27) exceeded the available budget by a greater margin than previously seen.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-08-19","committee":"Executive Cabinet Member Highways, Transport, Planning and Development","paper_title":"Highways Capital Programme from 2026-2027 and year end outturn of the 2025-2026 Programme","paper_url":"https://bolton.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s16157/16157.pdf","headline":"2025/26 Maintenance Block overspent; additional funding drawn from other sources to cover variance","detail":"The highways maintenance budget exceeded its allocation during 2025/26 due to unprogrammed reactive maintenance schemes, requiring the council to reallocate funds from other areas to cover the shortfall.","category":"overspend","matters":"Material maintenance budget overruns indicate either deteriorating asset condition driving reactive work, or poor budget forecasting; either suggests future budget pressures.","figures":"2025/26 Maintenance Block overspend; amount unspecified but significant enough to require reallocation","quote":"The impact of unprogrammed reactive schemes is that the 2025/26 Maintenance Block budget was overspent and required additional funding from other sources.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-08-19","committee":"Executive Cabinet Member Highways, Transport, Planning and Development","paper_title":"Highways Capital Programme from 2026-2027 and year end outturn of the 2025-2026 Programme","paper_url":"https://bolton.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s16157/16157.pdf","headline":"Bridge and structures asset management budgets acknowledged as inadequate for anything beyond emergency repairs","detail":"The council explicitly states it cannot fund preventative maintenance on highway structures and bridges, only reactive maintenance and emergency works. This forces a reactive posture on critical infrastructure with deferred asset renewal.","category":"capital","matters":"Chronic underfunding of preventative bridge maintenance creates a deteriorating asset base and eventual need for emergency capital spending or service disruption.","figures":"Bridge maintenance budget of \u00a31053k against a forward programme covering 8+ years of works, with inadequacy acknowledged","quote":"Despite utilising an electronic bridge management system, asset management for highway structures and the forward planning of workloads continues to be extremely challenging because budgets are inadequate to cover anything beyond reactive maintenance and emergency works.","material":false},{"iso":"2026-06-24","committee":"Audit Committee","paper_title":"24.6.26 Audit Committee Risk Update June 2026 Appendix","paper_url":"https://bolton.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s15686/15686.pdf","headline":"Escalating children's services placement costs with no funded solution, may force use of unregulated providers","detail":"Corporate Risk CR6 flags increasing costs of placements for children with complex needs transitioning to adulthood, limited availability of suitable options. Risk rated High (4x3=12). The council faces inability to provide appropriate placements and risk of forced use of unregulated providers with potential legal and reputational damage.","category":"demand","matters":"This is unfunded demand inflation in a statutory service; it signals a structural gap between needs and resources that must show up as either service rationing or deficit.","figures":"Risk rating 4x3=12 (High); 6-month target 4x3=12 (High)","quote":"Increasing costs of placements for children transitioning into adulthood, driven by higher needs including the complex care needs of some service users and limited availability of suitable options, could result the Council being unable to provide appropriate placements, sustain current arrangements or have no choice but to use unregulated providers.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-06-24","committee":"Audit Committee","paper_title":"24.6.26 Audit Committee Risk Update June 2026 Appendix","paper_url":"https://bolton.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s15686/15686.pdf","headline":"Workforce turnover and recruitment costs rising in competitive labour market with ageing workforce","detail":"Corporate Risk CR4 flags turnover and recruitment challenges in a competitive job market and ageing workforce driving increased operational costs (agency and recruitment) and service delivery risk. Rated High (4x3=12) with no expected improvement.","category":"overspend","matters":"Uncontrolled agency and recruitment cost inflation is a live driver of budget overrun; the council's stated appetite to operate 'below target capacity' signals it expects permanent structural underfunding.","figures":"Risk rating 4x3=12 (High); 6-month target 4x3=12 (High)","quote":"Due to turnover and recruitment challenges in a competitive job market and ageing workforce there is a risk of further reduced capability and capacity leading to increased operational costs (agency & recruitment costs) and risk to service delivery.","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-08-19"},"name":"Bolton","findings_total":37,"news_drafts_total":3}}